# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values.
# .env is gitignored. This file is committed. Never put a real secret here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT uses the PostgreSQL 18 Windows service, which starts
# with the machine and needs nothing started by hand.
#
# It has its own database and role so this project cannot reach the
# other databases on that server. Create them once with:
#
#     psql -U postgres -c "CREATE ROLE queen_bees WITH LOGIN PASSWORD 'queen_bees_dev'"
#     psql -U postgres -c "CREATE DATABASE queen_bees OWNER queen_bees"
#
# then: npx prisma@7 db push && npx prisma@7 generate && npx prisma@7 db seed
#
# This url is local only and is not a secret.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://queen_bees:queen_bees_dev@localhost:5432/queen_bees"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRODUCTION database (Neon, ap-southeast-1 Singapore)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Production credentials NEVER go in this file or in .env. They live in
# two places only:
#
#   1. The deployment host's environment variables, for the running app.
#   2. .env.neon on your machine, for schema pushes. Gitignored.
#
# Neon gives two connection strings for the SAME database and they are
# not interchangeable. Both come from the Connect button in the Neon
# dashboard; the toggle decides which one you get.
#
#   POOLED   Connection pooling ON.  Host contains "-pooler".
#            -> DATABASE_URL, in the DEPLOYMENT HOST only.
#            The running application uses this. It survives many
#            concurrent serverless requests.
#
#   DIRECT   Connection pooling OFF. Host has no "-pooler".
#            -> DIRECT_DATABASE_URL, in .env.neon only.
#            Schema pushes use this. The pooler runs PgBouncer in
#            transaction mode and cannot execute their statements.
#
# So .env.neon contains exactly one line:
#
#     DIRECT_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://neondb_owner:...@ep-...ap-southeast-1.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require"
#
# Then push the schema to production with the production config, which
# is the ONLY command that can reach it:
#
#     npx prisma@7 db push --config prisma-prod.config.ts
#
# A plain `npx prisma@7 db push` always goes to the local database. That
# is deliberate: reaching production has to be something you typed on
# purpose. The production config refuses a pooled url and refuses
# localhost, and prints the host it is about to touch.
#
# Never run `db seed` against production. The seed writes a development
# admin password that is published in prisma/seed.ts.
#
# Neon's free plan suspends the compute when idle, so the first query
# after a quiet period takes about half a second longer. That is normal.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Razorpay
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# From the Razorpay dashboard, Settings then API Keys. Use the TEST keys
# until the client is ready to take real money.
#
# RAZORPAY_KEY_ID is safe in the browser and is the only one of these
# three that may ever be sent to the client.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID="rzp_test_REPLACE_ME"

# Server only. Signs and verifies payments. If this leaks, anyone can
# forge a paid booking. Never prefix it NEXT_PUBLIC_ and never import it
# into a "use client" file.
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET="REPLACE_ME"

# Server only. Set when creating the webhook in the Razorpay dashboard.
# Webhook signatures are verified against this, not against the key
# secret above.
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET="REPLACE_ME"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Admin session
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Encrypts the admin session cookie. Must be at least 32 characters.
# Generate one with:
#
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
# Changing this logs every admin out. Server only.
SESSION_SECRET="REPLACE_ME_WITH_32_PLUS_RANDOM_CHARACTERS"
